    slug: "paypal-race-condition-79",
    shortDescription: "Explain a race condition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA race condition occurs when the result depends on the timing of concurrent operations on shared state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the protocol, operating-system, or security boundary rather than only knowing the terminology. The interviewer is usually looking for flow, failure behavior, and the reason for the guarantee.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the protocol or system behavior.\n2. Trace the relevant flow or state transition.\n3. State the guarantee and what it does not guarantee.\n4. Mention the failure or security consequence that matters.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the boundary or protocol definition, then walk through the flow. After that I would state what guarantee the mechanism provides and one important limitation or failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nTwo requests read balance=100, both add 10, and both write 110; one update is lost because the read-modify-write sequence was not coordinated.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nRace conditions matter anywhere shared state is updated concurrently; atomics, locks, transactions, or immutable designs can provide the required ordering or isolation.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing layers, such as transport behavior with application behavior.\n- Treating a security control as a complete security solution.\n- Ignoring failure, timing, or concurrency.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
